<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Merged into the generated Info.plist by Tauri.

    The studio spawns the opencode sidecar with its working directory set to
    the fork checkout. In development that checkout lives under ~/Documents,
    which macOS protects with TCC. A GUI (LaunchServices) launch has its own
    TCC identity and no inherited grant, so the child blocks in getcwd() and
    the sidecar never starts — the window shows "sidecar unreachable" with
    nothing in any log. `tauri dev` and running Contents/MacOS/downes-studio
    from a terminal both inherit Terminal's existing grant, so the failure is
    invisible there and appears only on a real double-click.

    Declaring the usage makes macOS prompt instead of silently blocking.

    Shipping note: a released build should not read the user's ~/Documents at
    all. The sidecar belongs inside the app bundle as a Tauri sidecar (board
    card: "compiled binary into packaging/CI"); once it does, this key covers
    only the case where a teacher keeps their studio in Documents.
  -->
  <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Downes needs access to load its curriculum engine and to read and write your course files.</string>
  <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Downes needs access if you keep your studio folder on the Desktop.</string>
  <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Downes needs access if you open course files from Downloads.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
